NAME
       chpasswd - set many passwords at once from standard input

SYNOPSIS
       chpasswd < FILE
       echo 'USER:PASSWORD' | chpasswd

DESCRIPTION
       chpasswd reads lines of the form user:password from standard input
       and sets each user's password, as passwd would. It is meant for
       scripts and for creating many accounts. It must be run as root.

       The password is given in clear text, so it may be seen in the shell
       history or in the process list. Remove such files afterwards.

EXAMPLES
       Set the password of one user without being asked:

           echo 'student:redhat' | chpasswd

       Create users from a list and give each the same first password:

           for u in alice bob carol; do
               useradd "$u"
               echo "$u:Welcome1" | chpasswd
               chage -d 0 "$u"
           done

SEE ALSO
       passwd(1), useradd(8), chage(1)
